# 23. Rule Classification Summary

| Layer | Approximate scope | Primary purpose |
|---|---:|---|
| Deterministic | ~40+ rules | Catch objective repository/content defects |
| AI-assisted | ~25+ rules | Surface semantic/content-quality issues |
| Human | Overlaps AI-assisted | Final judgment and approval |
| Deferred | Selected checks | Future capability after evidence/infrastructure exists |

The numbers are approximate because several rules intentionally have split responsibilities.

---

# 24. QA Decision Model

The eventual system should distinguish:

### PASS

No blocking deterministic failures.

### WARNING

A non-blocking issue exists.

### NEEDS HUMAN REVIEW

AI/heuristic review found an issue or the content contains a claim that requires human verification.

### FAIL

A deterministic HARD rule is violated.

A module can therefore have:

> **PASS + NEEDS HUMAN REVIEW**

That means structural QA passed, but the content is not automatically considered publish-ready.

---

# 25. What We Must NOT Build

The first QA system should not:

- use an LLM for every file just to validate YAML
- block PRs based on subjective AI opinions
- invent a new module schema
- require identical headings across module families
- require exactly five lab tasks
- require every optional section
- declare technical accuracy from keyword presence
- treat URL reachability as resource quality
- automatically rewrite failing modules
- execute arbitrary security commands
- auto-publish content
- auto-merge PRs

